Simpler robust approach: **suppress if the unity predicate is under the scope of a mention-inducing operator**. Operators:
- Reporting/attitude verbs with sentential complement: say(s), report(s), claim(s), assert(s), state(s), argue(s), note(s), question(s), whether, ask(s), reject(s), deny/denies, refute(s), dismiss(es), quote(s), flag(s), label(s), describe(s), mention(s), discuss(es), audit(s), test(s)? (no — FN2 "We test that X is the right unit" should fire), match(es)? ("pattern matches only bare composites" — no sentential complement of unity).
- Meta-nouns governing a complement: claim that, question whether, reading, pattern, finding that, assertion that (with quantifier 0/genuine/count), "the question whether", "disposition", "label".

Hmm, M7 "0 genuine assertions that C0 and C2 are one class" has a unity predicate in a complement under meta-noun "assertions that" + count 0 → mention. But A07 "The assertion is simple: C0 and C2 are one class" has meta-noun "assertion" in a *different clause* (colon split) → the second clause is standalone → assertion. And M02 "rejects any claim that C0/C2 form one class" → matrix verb rejects + claim that → mention. GM2 "The detector finding that C0/C2 are one class is rejected." → matrix subject "finding that" + predicate "is rejected" → mention.

So the rule: **within a clause**, if the composite+unity-predicate is embedded under a matrix predicate that is non-veridical/negative (reject, deny, question, whether, quote, flag, label, describe, discuss, mention, count, test?, match) or under a meta-noun with a negative/counting quantifier ("no genuine assertion", "0 ... assertions") → mention. If the matrix predicate is veridical/endorsing (agree, conclude, hold, decision, adopt, keep, treat, consider, unit, position) → assertion.

Distinguishing "We test that the C0/C2 unified class is the right unit" (fire) from "The detector finding that C0/C2 are one class is rejected" (silent): matrix predicate "test" is veridical-ish (they assert the content), "is rejected" is negative. So classify by the matrix predicate's polarity/veridicality — implement as lists:
- SUPPRESS_MATRIX = reject|deny|refute|dismiss|question|whether|ask|quote|flag|label|describe|report|discuss|mention|match|count|measure|audit|disposition|false positive|non-merge|split|separate|distinct|stale|irrelevant|retired|moved|no genuine|0 |zero |not the case
- ASSERT_MATRIX = agree|conclude|hold|decide|decision|adopt|keep|treat|consider|assert (bare)|maintain|endorse|position|unit|right unit|design|schema|class(?!...)|merged|unified|combined...

The "verb + complement" structure is the key. Without a parser, I'll approximate with ordered regexes over the clause and clause-neighborhood. This is a genuine structural prototype (clause-level), even if implemented with regexes.

